{{- /*
Per-language notes, looked up as "notes:<variant>" then "notes:<code>"
(variant = Lang.Variant(): zh-Hans, zh-Hant, pt-BR, es-MX, en-GB…).
Only languages with conventions worth stating have notes.
*/ -}}

{{define "notes:zh-Hans" -}}
- Write Simplified Chinese characters as used in Mainland China.
- Use full-width Chinese punctuation (，。！？：；“”……——) and no spaces between Chinese characters.
- Prefer spoken Mandarin over literary phrasing; avoid translationese such as stacked "的" or "被" passives.
- Leave a Latin-script name or term as is when that is how Chinese speakers say it (e.g. iPhone, Wi-Fi).
{{- end}}

{{define "notes:zh-Hant" -}}
- Write Traditional Chinese characters{{if eq .Region "HK"}} with Hong Kong vocabulary and usage{{else}} with Taiwan vocabulary and usage (e.g. 影片, 軟體, 資訊){{end}}.
- Use full-width punctuation (，。！？：；「」……——) and no spaces between Chinese characters.
- Prefer natural spoken phrasing over literary phrasing.
{{- end}}

{{define "notes:ja" -}}
- Use natural spoken Japanese with 、 and 。; no spaces between words.
- Keep each character's politeness level (です/ます vs. plain form) consistent with the scene and the speaker relationships shown in the context.
- Use katakana for foreign names; drop pronouns where Japanese would.
{{- end}}

{{define "notes:ko" -}}
- Use natural spoken Korean with standard spacing.
- Keep speech levels (해요체 / 합니다체 / 반말) consistent with the speaker relationships shown in the context.
{{- end}}

{{define "notes:es" -}}
- Use neutral international Spanish unless a region is given; use ¿…? and ¡…!.
{{- end}}

{{define "notes:es-ES" -}}
- Use Castilian Spanish as spoken in Spain, including "vosotros" for plural "you" and Spain-specific vocabulary (ordenador, móvil, coche).
- Use ¿…? and ¡…!.
{{- end}}

{{define "notes:es-MX" -}}
- Use Mexican Spanish: "ustedes" for plural "you", Mexican vocabulary (computadora, celular, carro) and natural Mexican colloquialisms where the register allows.
- Use ¿…? and ¡…!.
{{- end}}

{{define "notes:es-419" -}}
- Use neutral Latin American Spanish: "ustedes" for plural "you", vocabulary understood across Latin America (computadora, celular), no strongly local slang.
- Use ¿…? and ¡…!.
{{- end}}

{{define "notes:pt-BR" -}}
- Use Brazilian Portuguese: "você" as the usual "you", Brazilian vocabulary and spelling (ônibus, celular, tela) and natural spoken constructions.
{{- end}}

{{define "notes:pt-PT" -}}
- Use European Portuguese: "tu"/"você" as used in Portugal, Portuguese vocabulary and spelling (autocarro, telemóvel, ecrã) and the infinitive construction (estou a fazer).
{{- end}}

{{define "notes:pt" -}}
- Use Brazilian Portuguese unless the style notes say otherwise.
{{- end}}

{{define "notes:en-GB" -}}
- Use British English spelling and vocabulary (colour, flat, lorry, mobile).
{{- end}}

{{define "notes:en-US" -}}
- Use American English spelling and vocabulary (color, apartment, truck, cell phone).
{{- end}}

{{define "notes:fr" -}}
- Use standard French with the usual spaces before ; : ! ? and « ».
- Choose "tu" or "vous" from the speaker relationships and keep it consistent.
{{- end}}

{{define "notes:fr-CA" -}}
- Use Canadian (Québec) French vocabulary and usage, without forcing strong joual unless the style notes ask for it.
- Choose "tu" or "vous" from the speaker relationships and keep it consistent.
{{- end}}

{{define "notes:de" -}}
- Choose "du" or "Sie" from the speaker relationships and keep it consistent; use „…“ quotation marks.
{{- end}}

{{define "notes:ar" -}}
- Use Modern Standard Arabic suitable for dubbing unless the style notes ask for a dialect; use Arabic punctuation (، ؟).
{{- end}}

{{define "notes:ru" -}}
- Choose "ты" or "вы" from the speaker relationships and keep it consistent; use «…» quotation marks.
{{- end}}
